I was wrong about the Hyperscale databases being small. I said their maxSizeGB of ~0 meant no size justification for Hyperscale. That reading was wrong. The value you saw was -9.31E-10, which is -1 ÷ 1073741824 — meaning maxSizeBytes = -1, Azure's way of saying "not applicable." For Hyperscale, maximum data storage size is set to 128 TB and isn't configurable, and you're charged only for allocated data storage, not maximum. So that field is meaningless for Hyperscale and tells you nothing about their real size. We still need to find that out.

PART 1 — The Resource Graph sheet

Your current query is missing four columns that turn out to matter a lot. Replace it with this:

resources
| where type == "microsoft.sql/servers/databases"
| where name != "master"
| extend server = tostring(split(id,'/')[8])
| extend
    tier            = tostring(sku.tier),
    skuName         = tostring(sku.name),
    capacity        = toint(sku.capacity),
    minCapacity     = todouble(properties.minCapacity),
    autoPauseDelay  = toint(properties.autoPauseDelay),
    maxSizeBytes    = tolong(properties.maxSizeBytes),
    status          = tostring(properties.status),
    createdTime     = todatetime(properties.creationDate),
    earliestRestore = todatetime(properties.earliestRestoreDate),
    licenseType     = tostring(properties.licenseType),
    backupRedundancy= tostring(properties.currentBackupStorageRedundancy),
    zoneRedundant   = tostring(properties.zoneRedundant),
    readScale       = tostring(properties.readScale),
    elasticPoolId   = tostring(properties.elasticPoolId),
    serviceObjective= tostring(properties.requestedServiceObjectiveName)
| extend maxSizeGB = iff(maxSizeBytes <= 0, real(null), maxSizeBytes/1073741824.0)
| extend capacityUnit = iff(tier in ("Basic","Standard","Premium"), "DTU", "vCore")
| project resourceGroup, server, name, status, tier, skuName, serviceObjective,
          capacity, capacityUnit, minCapacity, autoPauseDelay,
          maxSizeGB, licenseType, backupRedundancy, zoneRedundant, readScale,
          elasticPoolId, createdTime, earliestRestore, tags, id
| order by capacity desc


Why each new column earns its place

licenseType — the single most valuable addition. LicenseIncluded means you're paying Microsoft for the SQL licence on that database. BasePrice means Azure Hybrid Benefit is already applied. This turns the ~£3,527/month licence meter from a lump sum into a per-database list of candidates.

backupRedundancy — you have RA-GRS (geo-redundant) backup charges in a non-production subscription. Every database showing Geo or GeoZone is paying a premium for cross-region backup redundancy that non-prod almost certainly doesn't need.

capacityUnit — solves the "800 vCores" misreading problem at source. Standard/Basic/Premium tiers report DTUs in the same field that vCore tiers use for cores. This column labels them correctly so nobody misreads your summary.

maxSizeGB with the null guard — iff(maxSizeBytes <= 0, null, ...) turns Hyperscale's -1 into a blank instead of that nonsense negative. A blank correctly says "not applicable here."

elasticPoolId — if any database sits in an elastic pool, its cost behaves completely differently (the pool is billed, not the database). You need to know before you analyse anything.

readScale — an enabled read replica means you're paying for a second copy of the compute.

earliestRestore — how far back point-in-time restore can reach. Relevant for anything you're considering deleting.

The storage usage problem — you're right

Resource Graph gives you maxSizeGB, which is the ceiling you provisioned. It cannot give you allocated or used space. Those live in Azure Monitor metrics, not in the resource properties.

Three quantities, all different:


	What it is	Where it lives
Max size	The ceiling you set	Resource Graph
Allocated	Disk space the files have claimed	Metric allocated_data_storage
Used	Data actually inside those files	Metric storage

The relationship is always used ≤ allocated ≤ max. And the file allocation doesn't shrink on its own, so allocated can sit high long after data was deleted.

This matters because you cannot reduce max size below allocated. So if a database shows 350 GB used but 600 GB allocated, your floor is 600 GB, not 350 — unless you shrink the files first.

Rather than querying each database by hand, run this once

$sub = "3cf5a9a2-bc13-43e2-b97e-357215ab3a5d"
$dbs = az graph query -q "resources
  | where type=='microsoft.sql/servers/databases'
  | where name != 'master'
  | project id, name, server=tostring(split(id,'/')[8])" `
  --first 1000 -o json | ConvertFrom-Json

$out = foreach ($db in $dbs.data) {
  try {
    $m = az monitor metrics list --resource $db.id `
      --metric "storage,allocated_data_storage" `
      --interval PT1H --aggregation Maximum `
      --start-time (Get-Date).AddDays(-2).ToString("yyyy-MM-ddTHH:mm:ssZ") `
      -o json 2>$null | ConvertFrom-Json

    $used = ($m.value | Where-Object name.value -eq 'storage').timeseries.data.maximum |
            Measure-Object -Maximum | Select-Object -Expand Maximum
    $alloc = ($m.value | Where-Object name.value -eq 'allocated_data_storage').timeseries.data.maximum |
            Measure-Object -Maximum | Select-Object -Expand Maximum

    [pscustomobject]@{
      server      = $db.server
      database    = $db.name
      usedGB      = [math]::Round($used/1GB, 2)
      allocatedGB = [math]::Round($alloc/1GB, 2)
    }
  } catch {
    [pscustomobject]@{ server=$db.server; database=$db.name; usedGB=$null; allocatedGB=$null }
  }
}
$out | Export-Csv storage-usage.csv -NoTypeInformation

⚠️ Two caveats. Inaccessible databases won't return metrics — expect blanks for the arc11x six, and don't read that as "zero storage." And paused serverless databases may not have emitted metrics recently, so widen to 7 days if you get gaps.

That gives you a third CSV to join on server / database.

