Finding 2 — Three Hyperscale databases that cannot pause (NOT STARTED)
Database	Server	Environment
iazsqldbdapdev	-sql-dap-dev	DEV
iazsqldbsit	-sql-sit-dap	SIT
iazsqldbuat	-sql-uat-dap	UAT

All three: HS_S_Gen5, max 40 vCores, min 5 vCores, autoPauseDelay = -1, status Online.

The structural problem: serverless auto-pausing is only supported in the General Purpose tier. So each bills a minimum of 5 vCores continuously, forever, and no setting will change that.

What I got wrong and you need to check: I claimed these were tiny databases. That was based on misreading the -1 size field. Their real size is unknown. Pull allocated_data_storage for all three — that number decides the entire finding. Multi-terabyte and Hyperscale is justified; a few hundred GB and it isn't.

Constraint if you do propose a move: reverse migration from Hyperscale to General Purpose is only supported within 45 days of the original migration to Hyperscale. These are well past that, so it's an export/import exercise, not a tier toggle. Cost that in.

Finding 3 — bstsitlarep: expensive by configuration (NOT STARTED)

~£4,867 in July — your second most expensive database.

GP_S_Gen5 · max 40 · min 20 vCores · autoPauseDelay 180 · 1,280 GB · Online.

Serverless, so in principle it should be cheap. But two settings defeat it:

min 20 vCores — a large guaranteed floor for every hour it's online
180-minute delay — needs three unbroken hours of zero activity to pause. In any environment with a monitoring agent or connection pool, that gap never occurs

This is the best value-for-effort item on the list. No tier change, no migration, no data movement — two numbers in the Compute + storage blade. Drop min toward 0.5 and the delay to 60, then watch a week of app_cpu_billed.

